window.docContents[25].resolve({"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L0-___-The-black-box--Pull":{"contents":"The oscillator is one body on an ideal, massless spring fixed to an infinitely massive wall, moving along a line. The body is homogeneous, rigid, and point-like; those assumptions are revisited at L3. Its bare-real model has no type-level record of what mass and stiffness mean and accepts the reciprocal-frequency expression √(m/k) where √(k/m) was intended. A dimension tag can catch dimensional mistakes but cannot distinguish kinds that share a dimension. The system context comes before the attributes it needs.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L0 — The black box","header":"5.1.3. Pull","id":"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L0-___-The-black-box--Pull"}});